1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Harvesting provides humans with over 2 billion tons

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:05,000
of fruit and vegetables every year.

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's one of the most crucial industries on the planet.

4
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:09,000
It's dangerous work up there.

5
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
One slip and they could end up in a world of hurt.

6
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
These high-tech wonders have transformed

7
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
America's 2 million farms into a $140 billion industry.

8
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
You're talking about the ability to harvest

9
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
4,000 or so bushels an hour?

10
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
From optical fruit sizers

11
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:28,000
to computer-controlled corn-picking combines,

12
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,340
You'll never shop for produce the same way again

13
00:00:31,341 --> 00:00:33,000
on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:51,000
Every year, from spring until fall,

15
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:53,500
California's fruit harvest is in high gear.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
At Reimer Farms in California's San Joaquin Valley,

17
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:04,000
they're picking nectarines.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
For all growers, stretching the harvest season means raising

19
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:13,000
different varieties of the same fruit.

20
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:16,000
We have varieties planted specifically throughout the

21
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:19,000
different times of the season to fulfill those different time

22
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:22,000
slots for providing fruit to the marketplace.

23
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:25,000
So a typical variety of nectarines may harvest for two

24
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:27,000
to three weeks.

25
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:32,000
A good harvest depends on fruit that looks and tastes its best.

26
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,600
To add maximum appeal to a nectarine,

27
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:39,940
most growers line the orchard rows with strips of mylar.

28
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:42,620
The reflective surface concentrates the sun's rays

29
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:46,280
into the tree canopy, promoting photosynthesis

30
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:49,520
and increasing the sugar content and color quality of the fruit.

31
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:52,020
And that's when the fruit's actually going through its sizing

32
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:53,020
process.

33
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:55,820
It's when it's developing its sugar, developing its color.

34
00:01:55,821 --> 00:01:58,460
and so we're trying to maximize its environment

35
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:02,460
during that particular critical time point before harvest.

36
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:04,860
As the workers begin picking,

37
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,840
growers will spot check the fruit to verify their decision

38
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,100
to start harvesting.

39
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,340
So what we'll do when we're trying to decide whether we're

40
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:16,440
harvesting properly is we'll sample them with a penetrometer

41
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:19,440
to see what the flesh firmness is to determine whether we are

42
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:21,240
picking the level we want.

43
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:24,520
So we just quickly just shave a piece of the skin off,

44
00:02:24,521 --> 00:02:27,760
and then we use this plunger to push in there,

45
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,020
and the force that it takes to push that in

46
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,260
will then determine the flesh firmness.

47
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:36,060
This one rated at 11.

48
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:39,160
Picking is no longer the last step in the harvest.

49
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:41,880
In many cases, it's just the beginning.

50
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:47,780
This 10-acre packing facility operates

51
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:50,920
three state-of-the-art packing lines for boxing peaches,

52
00:02:50,921 --> 00:02:56,420
peaches, plums, blueberries, apples, and many other fruits.

53
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:02,260
Today, it's nectarines and pomegranates.

54
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:09,340
In one shift, the workers can pack up to 120,000 boxes

55
00:03:09,341 --> 00:03:13,880
of fruit on two lines, all of it sorted according to size,

56
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:16,580
color, and degree of ripeness.

57
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:20,900
We become almost the warehouse for the grocery store.

58
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:23,620
So they'll give us an order and give us only like a one or two

59
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:26,120
day lead time to pack it, put it on a truck,

60
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:29,620
and get it over to anywhere in the United States.

61
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:31,560
Once a piece of fruit is picked,

62
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:36,940
its limited lifespan sparks the race to preserve freshness.

63
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,540
Here, sophisticated post-harvest handling

64
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:45,140
is a way to stall for time, starting with a cold shower.

65
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:48,840
The hydro cooler pours frigid 32-degree water over the fruit

66
00:03:48,841 --> 00:03:51,300
over the fruit to reduce its internal temperature

67
00:03:51,301 --> 00:03:52,540
and slow down decay.

68
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:56,900
This is absolutely crucial in maintaining good quality.

69
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,220
In the harvest season in California,

70
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:00,300
in the summertime, our temperatures

71
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:02,520
can be well over 90 degrees Fahrenheit,

72
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:04,120
even 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

73
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,920
And the fruit will lose quality quite quickly,

74
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,180
will dry and lose sugars.

75
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:14,220
The cooled fruit quickly makes its way

76
00:04:14,221 --> 00:04:17,000
onto the sorting line for a quick wash and wax.

77
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:22,640
After it's rinsed, it's sprayed with a vegetable oil product

78
00:04:22,641 --> 00:04:25,140
to help prevent moisture loss during the long journey

79
00:04:25,141 --> 00:04:26,140
to the supermarket.

80
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:34,680
Before sizing begins, inspectors use their highly trained senses

81
00:04:34,681 --> 00:04:36,600
to remove the subpar fruit.

82
00:04:36,601 --> 00:04:39,800
As they touch it, they're looking for a soft shoulder

83
00:04:39,801 --> 00:04:44,600
or soft tip on the fruit that would bruise or damage easily

84
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:46,800
and could later decay in transit.

85
00:04:46,801 --> 00:04:50,240
in transit, so that piece of fruit that's a little bit too

86
00:04:50,241 --> 00:04:51,440
soft is then removed.

87
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,720
The rejects are diverted and will later produce juices,

88
00:04:57,721 --> 00:04:59,580
jams, and other processed products.

89
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:06,860
The fruit that passes inspection continues its journey

90
00:05:06,861 --> 00:05:08,260
to the high-speed sorting line.

91
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,060
For centuries, farmers have sorted fruit

92
00:05:13,061 --> 00:05:15,420
to achieve uniform shipping weights

93
00:05:15,421 --> 00:05:19,120
and to satisfy consumer preference.

94
00:05:19,121 --> 00:05:22,000
The first packing houses used crude sizers

95
00:05:22,001 --> 00:05:25,660
with wooden slats or holes for which the smaller sized fruits

96
00:05:25,661 --> 00:05:26,361
were dropped.

97
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:33,940
Today, the optical fruit sizer is a faster, more accurate method.

98
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:40,180
The fruit is first diverted onto a single file cup conveyor,

99
00:05:40,181 --> 00:05:44,240
moving at about 10 pieces per second.

100
00:05:44,241 --> 00:05:47,540
As it passes into the sorter, each piece is rotated

101
00:05:47,541 --> 00:05:50,120
and photographed by two lenses.

102
00:05:50,121 --> 00:05:53,960
An infrared lens gathers shape and dimensional data,

103
00:05:53,961 --> 00:05:58,160
and a color lens collects the color qualities of the fruit.

104
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:00,720
The camera quickly snaps 25 photos

105
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:04,880
to create a two-dimensional projection of the entire fruit.

106
00:06:04,881 --> 00:06:07,200
From this picture, the computer interprets

107
00:06:07,201 --> 00:06:10,600
its exact size, shape, and color.

108
00:06:10,601 --> 00:06:11,960
Now we've got a full look at it,

109
00:06:11,961 --> 00:06:14,060
and then we can determine if the amount of color

110
00:06:14,061 --> 00:06:16,360
red versus yellow is the appropriate color

111
00:06:16,361 --> 00:06:17,900
that's desirable for packing.

112
00:06:17,901 --> 00:06:21,200
And that's helpful in markets where we want to send fruit

113
00:06:21,201 --> 00:06:25,400
of a uniform size, color, and shape.

114
00:06:25,401 --> 00:06:27,700
Once the computer obtains the mugshots

115
00:06:27,701 --> 00:06:30,040
of a certain piece of fruit, a timing device

116
00:06:30,041 --> 00:06:33,040
triggers a lifting finger that deposits the fruit

117
00:06:33,041 --> 00:06:38,060
into a specific lane according to size and color.

118
00:06:38,061 --> 00:06:41,480
This high-tech sorter delivers nearly identical fruit

119
00:06:41,481 --> 00:06:45,480
fruit to each of many packing lines.

120
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:48,780
After a final human inspection, the fruit is hand-packed in a

121
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:50,480
variety of boxes.

122
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:57,520
Then it's conveyed by forklift into a cold storage room where

123
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:00,340
it's stored at an ideal or optimum temperature that

124
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:03,140
actually puts the fruit to sleep.

125
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:05,980
Once the fruit is in that condition or in that state,

126
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,280
it's in the best environment to be transported from California

127
00:07:09,281 --> 00:07:11,780
markets in the Midwest or the East Coast.

128
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:16,880
The packing house can even hold boxed fruit

129
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:19,220
at a higher temperature to force ripening.

130
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,820
Sophisticated post-harvest handling of fruit is a

131
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:29,040
progression and a ritual that has remained otherwise unchanged

132
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,640
over thousands of years.

133
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:35,640
This Egyptian tomb drawing, dated at 1900 BCE,

134
00:07:35,641 --> 00:07:38,640
depicts two workers harvesting a fig tree.

135
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:41,520
The key components of harvest are the same.

136
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:44,880
One's picking the fruit and then putting it into a basket.

137
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:48,280
The other is sorting the fruit and putting it

138
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:50,380
into a consumer package.

139
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:54,360
The practices involved in picking and packing

140
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:58,500
are basically still the same and haven't changed.

141
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,640
Ancient Egypt is one of the birthplaces

142
00:08:04,641 --> 00:08:05,640
of horticulture.

143
00:08:05,641 --> 00:08:11,640
The first growers learned to irrigate their fields by tapping the Nile River's seasonal

144
00:08:11,641 --> 00:08:12,640
floods.

145
00:08:12,641 --> 00:08:19,640
But without modern technologies, these growers were faced with the problem of spoilage.

146
00:08:19,641 --> 00:08:24,640
They didn't have refrigeration, they didn't have canning, they didn't have these other

147
00:08:24,641 --> 00:08:28,640
processes to preserve the harvested crop.

148
00:08:28,641 --> 00:08:33,640
So the crop had to either be consumed fresh soon after harvest

149
00:08:33,641 --> 00:08:38,640
or dried and stored dry.

150
00:08:38,641 --> 00:08:40,640
China was the birthplace of temperate fruits,

151
00:08:40,641 --> 00:08:44,640
such as peaches and pears, more than 4,000 years ago.

152
00:08:44,641 --> 00:08:47,640
The Greeks and Romans oversaw a thriving fruit trade

153
00:08:47,641 --> 00:08:50,640
throughout the Mediterranean.

154
00:08:50,641 --> 00:08:53,640
When Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE,

155
00:08:53,641 --> 00:08:56,640
the Romans were cultivating extensive orchards,

156
00:08:56,641 --> 00:08:59,640
including peaches, citrus trees, and grapes

157
00:08:59,641 --> 00:09:03,140
on the volcano's slopes.

158
00:09:03,141 --> 00:09:05,140
During the Middle Ages, monks were

159
00:09:05,141 --> 00:09:07,140
the most sophisticated growers.

160
00:09:07,141 --> 00:09:10,640
They practiced the skills of tree grafting and crossbreeding

161
00:09:10,641 --> 00:09:14,640
to produce new varieties of fruit trees.

162
00:09:14,641 --> 00:09:17,140
The colonists brought Europe's fruit tree saplings

163
00:09:17,141 --> 00:09:20,640
to the New World, but the true American orchard revolution

164
00:09:20,641 --> 00:09:25,640
was again fueled by monks.

165
00:09:25,641 --> 00:09:27,880
As the Spanish moved north into California

166
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:30,280
and established missions in the 1600s,

167
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,880
the monks took advantage of the dry climate

168
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:37,080
and raised large orchards of grapes, figs, peaches, pears,

169
00:09:37,180 --> 00:09:38,880
and apricots.

170
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:42,400
As settlers moved to California in the 1800s,

171
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:45,700
the orchards fanned out to supply the new urban centers

172
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:49,300
of Los Angeles and San Francisco.

173
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:51,740
Growers also planted in the Sierra foothills

174
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:54,940
to feed the influx of gold miners.

175
00:09:54,941 --> 00:09:58,940
California fruit production exploded in the 1890s

176
00:09:58,941 --> 00:10:02,940
when refrigerated railroad cars allowed growers to ship fresh fruit

177
00:10:02,941 --> 00:10:05,940
to the huge East Coast markets.

178
00:10:05,941 --> 00:10:09,940
But in the coming decades, the small family-operated farm

179
00:10:09,941 --> 00:10:11,940
would nearly vanish.

180
00:10:11,941 --> 00:10:15,940
Farms began to get bigger primarily because of competition.

181
00:10:15,941 --> 00:10:20,940
There's a continuing pressure to reduce the price of fruits

182
00:10:20,941 --> 00:10:23,940
and vegetables at retail market.

183
00:10:23,941 --> 00:10:27,940
And that means the farms generally have to get larger in size

184
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:29,940
to take advantage of economies of scale.

185
00:10:33,940 --> 00:10:36,940
Large, vertically integrated growing and packing operations

186
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:39,940
are now the standard in U.S. fruit production.

187
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:43,940
But labor remains the most unpredictable and expensive cost

188
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:47,940
for growers, especially when balancing the need to pay a decent

189
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:51,940
wage while providing affordable produce for consumers.

190
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:58,940
Beginning in the 1960s, engineers from the University

191
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:02,940
of California at Davis began testing a multitude of mechanical

192
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:03,940
fruit harvesters.

193
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,880
Various tree shakers and shake and catch machines were very

194
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:11,900
effective at harvesting fruit quickly.

195
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:17,400
But the percentage of damaged fruit was unacceptable.

196
00:11:17,401 --> 00:11:18,101
acceptable.

197
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:23,900
Half a century later, there are still

198
00:11:23,901 --> 00:11:26,320
no commercially viable mechanical harvesters

199
00:11:26,321 --> 00:11:28,780
for picking many types of fresh fruit.

200
00:11:28,781 --> 00:11:31,740
One solution may be a man-machine interface

201
00:11:31,741 --> 00:11:34,520
that allows laborers to more efficiently access the tree

202
00:11:34,521 --> 00:11:35,221
canopy.

203
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:42,200
Here, a pair grower and engineers from UC Davis

204
00:11:42,201 --> 00:11:44,660
are testing a modified European-built platform

205
00:11:44,661 --> 00:11:45,700
in Northern California.

206
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:51,400
The machine offers six movable picking platforms

207
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:57,040
at three different heights, allowing the harvesters

208
00:11:57,140 --> 00:12:01,440
to move laterally or up and down inside the tree canopy.

209
00:12:01,540 --> 00:12:04,380
So the workers, instead of placing fruit into bags

210
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:07,060
and delivering it to bins, would place fruit two or three

211
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:10,520
at a time into little feeder conveyors that fed into a main

212
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:11,320
elevator.

213
00:12:11,321 --> 00:12:15,220
elevator.

214
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:17,820
At the top of the main elevator, the fruit would go

215
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:20,360
into what's called a bin filler, where the fruit is

216
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:23,540
lowered and placed into a rotating bin, which

217
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:26,200
ensures even distribution of the fruit in the bin.

218
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:32,880
By eliminating the need to carry heavy ladders

219
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:36,180
and picking bags, the platform might expand the potential

220
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:38,180
workforce.

221
00:12:38,181 --> 00:12:40,580
It could also improve employee safety

222
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:43,020
by reducing hazardous working conditions

223
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:46,120
and injuries from ladder falls, a major problem

224
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:46,920
for many growers.

225
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:52,320
The harvesting platform has already

226
00:12:52,420 --> 00:12:55,400
gained a firm footing in Europe, where hand labor has been

227
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:56,900
in short supply for decades.

228
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:05,940
In the Midwest, America's biggest harvest

229
00:13:05,941 --> 00:13:09,140
has eliminated handpicking and put the farmer at the wheel

230
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:12,340
of a computer-controlled, maximum efficiency machine.

231
00:13:16,780 --> 00:13:19,880
Nebraskan John Sandahl is harvesting corn

232
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:21,980
in a John Deere 12-row combine.

233
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:28,700
The air-conditioned cab may seem cushy,

234
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:32,040
and combining corn may look a bit like mowing the lawn.

235
00:13:32,140 --> 00:13:34,440
But this harvest demands a sharp eye

236
00:13:34,441 --> 00:13:35,800
and fast decision-making.

237
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:41,060
Well, harvesting corn, we're scanning the corn head all the time,

238
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:42,660
watching for objects in the field.

239
00:13:43,260 --> 00:13:45,940
Make sure that all the row units are functioning properly,

240
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:47,600
pulling the corn stalks down through.

241
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:52,420
And this corner post monitors all the functions of the machine.

242
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:54,880
The first one down here has the engine RPM,

243
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:56,480
the speed that I'm traveling.

244
00:13:57,720 --> 00:13:59,880
The middle one is the grain loss monitor.

245
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:01,360
That's very important.

246
00:14:01,361 --> 00:14:07,200
And it keeps track, monitors how much grain the combine is losing out the back.

247
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:12,820
A small percentage of the harvested corn drops back in the field.

248
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:17,440
And the operator minimizes these losses by slowing down when necessary.

249
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:22,440
The modern combine is a factory on wheels.

250
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:29,060
As corn stalks enter the corn head, a series of rollers pull down the stalks and snap off the ears.

251
00:14:29,061 --> 00:14:31,460
An auger moves the ears into the combine,

252
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:35,800
where they're conveyed into the threshing mechanism.

253
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:38,600
Here, a spinning rotor threshes the kernels,

254
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:42,400
removing them from the cob, while a strong fan blows out

255
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:44,300
the leaves and chaff.

256
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:47,620
The kernels are then conveyed to the combine's holding tank.

257
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:57,760
On Sandahl's farm, the corn is offloaded onto trailers.

258
00:14:57,761 --> 00:15:01,060
The kernels are loaded into bins that will store the grain

259
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:02,560
and complete the drying process.

260
00:15:05,060 --> 00:15:09,500
Corn is a crop with many uses, everything from tortillas

261
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:13,500
to corn syrup, animal feed, and ethanol,

262
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:16,480
a corn-based alternative fuel that is boosting demand

263
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:17,720
across the Corn Belt.

264
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:25,820
More than 7,000 years ago, it was a decidedly simpler crop.

265
00:15:25,821 --> 00:15:27,820
In the region that is now Mexico,

266
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:33,320
natives began harvesting a wild grass called Teosinte.

267
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:36,160
Over thousands of years, the Mesoamericans

268
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:38,860
domesticated Teosinte by sowing and reaping

269
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:41,760
only the best kernels.

270
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:44,880
It evolved into a hardier, more productive plant,

271
00:15:44,980 --> 00:15:48,780
known as maize, or corn.

272
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:52,820
After its first migration north at about 1200 BCE,

273
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:55,820
it would become the most important crop in America

274
00:15:55,821 --> 00:15:56,820
agriculture.

275
00:15:56,920 --> 00:15:59,720
When the pilgrims first arrived, they planted in small hills,

276
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:01,420
several seeds in a hill.

277
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:03,560
They grew the corn, they harvested the ears,

278
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:04,620
and they ground it in the flower.

279
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:10,560
In the colonies, and for centuries that followed,

280
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:12,060
corn was harvested by hand.

281
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,980
The corn knife, with an 18-inch blade,

282
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:22,480
was one of the original methods of cutting the stalks.

283
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:24,920
When farmers were picking only the ears,

284
00:16:24,921 --> 00:16:28,360
Handheld hooks, or pegs made of iron and bone,

285
00:16:28,361 --> 00:16:29,960
were the standard tools.

286
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:35,740
This is a bone peg that was probably used in 1890.

287
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:38,740
They used anything that was sharp

288
00:16:38,741 --> 00:16:40,860
to open up the shucks at that time.

289
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,720
Gloves with embedded hooks later replaced the peg.

290
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:48,040
People in rural communities are very innovative,

291
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,280
and so they were always looking for some way

292
00:16:50,281 --> 00:16:52,820
to do it better, to do it faster.

293
00:16:52,821 --> 00:16:56,120
The hooks and pegs allowed for faster harvesting

294
00:16:56,121 --> 00:16:59,520
because they could rip the shuck away from the ear,

295
00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:04,400
snap it, and harvest it much quicker than just by hand.

296
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:09,000
Even today, many rural communities

297
00:17:09,001 --> 00:17:11,140
still celebrate the days of hand harvesting

298
00:17:11,141 --> 00:17:13,000
with husking bee competitions.

299
00:17:15,740 --> 00:17:18,800
Ted Martin is a former Nebraska state champion.

300
00:17:19,380 --> 00:17:22,560
Well, you grab the ear towards the end like this,

301
00:17:22,820 --> 00:17:26,720
and come in with the hook, crossways, open up the shucks,

302
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:28,380
then you peel them back,

303
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:31,620
and you snap the ear out and throw it.

304
00:17:32,560 --> 00:17:35,480
And it's all one motion, really.

305
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:43,400
A fast husker could generally harvest about 100 bushels,

306
00:17:43,620 --> 00:17:45,400
or one acre of corn, per day.

307
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:50,600
But enormous advancements in agricultural technology

308
00:17:50,601 --> 00:17:53,340
allows modern farmers to dwarf this number.

309
00:17:56,180 --> 00:17:58,160
Now we return to the fields,

310
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:00,980
here on Modern Marvel's Harvesting Plus.

311
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:05,760
Tillers, combines, cultivators

312
00:18:05,761 --> 00:18:09,200
are just some of the innovative pieces of farming gear

313
00:18:09,201 --> 00:18:12,060
that have revolutionized harvesting in the modern era.

314
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:16,480
But long before machines like tractors and harvesters took over,

315
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:20,260
farming meant putting your shoulder to the wheel.

316
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,940
For decades, nearly every farm was equipped

317
00:18:23,040 --> 00:18:26,640
with a hand-operated corn sheller.

318
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:29,040
OK, we've got a couple cobs of corn.

319
00:18:29,140 --> 00:18:32,240
And we're looking at a hand-powered corn sheller

320
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:33,740
that's about 100 years old.

321
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:36,140
And we're going to run a couple cobs through it.

322
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:37,760
First, I'm going to crank it up.

323
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:41,660
A couple plates inside the machine going in opposite directions

324
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:43,960
are going to shear the kernels off.

325
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:49,860
The corn kernels go down the bottom.

326
00:18:49,861 --> 00:18:52,760
the corn cobs come out the other end.

327
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:55,860
And there's a fan at the bottom that blows some of the chaff

328
00:18:55,960 --> 00:18:57,260
away as well.

329
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:03,200
Many farmers harvested corn for silage, or animal feed.

330
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:05,800
This included the stalk as well as the ear.

331
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:12,120
Silage was the first corn harvest to see mechanization

332
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:15,120
in the form of the corn binder in the late 1800s.

333
00:19:15,121 --> 00:19:18,360
1800s.

334
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:22,520
On one Nebraska farm, Ron Lang operates an antique corn

335
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:26,660
binder to harvest feed for his horses.

336
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:29,860
It's an international corn binder.

337
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:33,580
It cuts it and ties it.

338
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:38,780
This mainly used to be used more for green corn and fill

339
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:40,380
silos with it.

340
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:43,120
They would cut the corn green and haul it in and fill your

341
00:19:43,220 --> 00:19:45,120
silos.

342
00:19:45,121 --> 00:19:48,920
The binder both cuts and then automatically ties the stalks

343
00:19:49,020 --> 00:19:51,760
into bundles with twine.

344
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:54,860
The operator uses a foot clutch to engage a conveyor

345
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:56,860
that drops the bundles in the field.

346
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:03,700
Corn binders were usually followed by a crew of laborers

347
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:06,780
who stood the bundles into larger upright stacks,

348
00:20:06,880 --> 00:20:09,120
known as shocks.

349
00:20:09,220 --> 00:20:12,020
According to Ron, this traditional shock building

350
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:15,120
technique prevents mold from growing in the bundles.

351
00:20:15,121 --> 00:20:19,120
which helps keep the feed from rotting.

352
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:21,120
Shalkin is for your winter feed,

353
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:24,560
and it would mold and stuff on the ground.

354
00:20:24,660 --> 00:20:26,160
And the shalkin is for curing.

355
00:20:26,260 --> 00:20:30,160
You've got to shock it so the wind goes through it.

356
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:32,080
Up through the Great Depression,

357
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:34,680
the meticulous hand harvest was still the only way

358
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:35,980
to pick the ears directly.

359
00:20:39,380 --> 00:20:41,480
The first mechanized corn pickers,

360
00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:43,720
powered by internal combustion engines,

361
00:20:43,721 --> 00:20:47,520
in the late 1930s and would soon double productivity

362
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,220
to 15 acres in one day.

363
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:59,260
The pickers selectively harvest only the corneers,

364
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:01,380
depositing the stalks back in the field.

365
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:09,320
Farmer Randy Jensen uses a two-row 1960s model 237 John

366
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:13,720
Deere picker to harvest 600 acres of corn each year.

367
00:21:13,721 --> 00:21:19,720
The picker uses rollers to snap the ears off the stalks.

368
00:21:19,721 --> 00:21:25,720
The ears travel to a husking bag, where rubber rollers remove the husks.

369
00:21:25,721 --> 00:21:28,720
The clean ears then drop in a wagon.

370
00:21:28,721 --> 00:21:35,720
When you come to the end, you'll have a full wagon most of the time, and you'll have a guy at the other end, and he'll switch wagons with you.

371
00:21:35,721 --> 00:21:41,720
And he'll take it home and run it to an elevator, which elevates it up into an ear corn crib.

372
00:21:41,721 --> 00:21:44,960
and basically that's all you do.

373
00:21:45,060 --> 00:21:47,820
While the mechanical pickers endured for decades,

374
00:21:47,920 --> 00:21:50,660
they couldn't shell clean and dry corn immediately

375
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:52,160
in the field.

376
00:21:52,260 --> 00:21:55,860
By the mid-1950s, there was an answer.

377
00:21:55,960 --> 00:21:59,100
The combine, which had long before mechanized the harvesting

378
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:01,880
of other grain crops, was now advanced enough

379
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:05,580
to produce clean corn kernels at a staggering pace.

380
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:11,480
What began with two row designs grew to be

381
00:22:11,481 --> 00:22:14,720
to the 12 and even 16 row corn head combines

382
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:16,780
of the 21st century.

383
00:22:16,880 --> 00:22:18,620
You're talking about the ability to harvest

384
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:23,320
4,000 or so bushels an hour, which translates to maybe 60

385
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:28,060
to 70 pounds of grain, clean grain, every second

386
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:30,840
that this machine can harvest.

387
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:33,900
If we go back to working with a corn binder,

388
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:37,680
we're talking about working maybe eight acres a day,

389
00:22:37,780 --> 00:22:40,100
and now we're talking about hand picking.

390
00:22:40,101 --> 00:22:43,440
We're talking an acre or so a day, 100 bushels.

391
00:22:43,540 --> 00:22:46,400
Today, big farmers with large combines

392
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:52,040
can do 40,000 bushels in a day with one combine.

393
00:22:52,140 --> 00:22:55,440
The American corn harvest relies on the size, speed,

394
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:57,460
and sophistication of one machine.

395
00:23:02,160 --> 00:23:05,300
Every year in October, there is a fruit that must first go

396
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:07,460
for a swim before it can be picked.

397
00:23:10,100 --> 00:23:14,100
Just days before harvesting, farmers in central Wisconsin

398
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:17,100
divert water from nearby reservoirs in order to flood

399
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:21,240
thousands of acres of cranberries in up to two feet of water.

400
00:23:21,340 --> 00:23:25,140
This is called a wet harvest.

401
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:28,660
The berries grow on short vines.

402
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:31,460
When they mature, the fields are flooded

403
00:23:31,560 --> 00:23:33,560
to make harvesting easier.

404
00:23:33,561 --> 00:23:35,560
to make harvesting easier.

405
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:42,560
These water reel harvesters, also called beaters,

406
00:23:42,561 --> 00:23:46,560
will transform the marsh into a sea of red.

407
00:23:46,561 --> 00:23:49,560
The first guy that goes in the water is opening the bed,

408
00:23:49,561 --> 00:23:51,560
and then the rest of the guys behind him

409
00:23:51,561 --> 00:23:53,560
just keep following along behind him.

410
00:23:53,561 --> 00:23:55,560
What they're looking for in the water

411
00:23:55,561 --> 00:23:57,560
is what we call a berry line,

412
00:23:57,561 --> 00:24:00,560
where you can see where the fruit's been picked clean

413
00:24:00,561 --> 00:24:02,560
and where the remaining fruit is.

414
00:24:02,561 --> 00:24:03,920
So you try to line up on that.

415
00:24:03,921 --> 00:24:07,440
And the idea is to not miss any fruit and beat it clean

416
00:24:07,441 --> 00:24:09,900
or pick it clean.

417
00:24:09,901 --> 00:24:11,800
As the beaters move through the vine,

418
00:24:11,801 --> 00:24:15,100
they strip off the berries.

419
00:24:15,101 --> 00:24:18,000
Cranberries contain four air chambers.

420
00:24:18,001 --> 00:24:20,620
When freed from the vines, their natural buoyancy

421
00:24:20,621 --> 00:24:22,520
causes them to float.

422
00:24:22,521 --> 00:24:25,280
Cranberries are trained, meaning the vines

423
00:24:25,281 --> 00:24:27,220
lay a certain direction.

424
00:24:27,221 --> 00:24:29,460
A good analogy would be like petting a cat the right way

425
00:24:29,461 --> 00:24:30,460
and the wrong way.

426
00:24:30,461 --> 00:24:31,720
You pet it the right way, everything's nice.

427
00:24:31,721 --> 00:24:33,720
against the vines or against that cat,

428
00:24:33,820 --> 00:24:35,060
and every fur gets all ruffled up,

429
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:36,460
and it's not a very nice thing.

430
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:38,460
Same thing can happen with cranberries.

431
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:41,160
If we go against the vines, we'd rip them out of the ground,

432
00:24:41,260 --> 00:24:42,160
and it would be a mess.

433
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:48,260
Once the three-acre bed is beaten,

434
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:51,880
the workers deploy a cran boom to slowly coax the flotilla

435
00:24:51,980 --> 00:24:53,080
to one end of the marsh.

436
00:24:57,780 --> 00:25:00,480
Here, more than 80,000 pounds of berries

437
00:25:00,481 --> 00:25:02,680
are loaded by elevator and trucked out.

438
00:25:07,780 --> 00:25:10,120
At the Ocean Spray Receiving Station,

439
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:18,240
the berries are offloaded into holding tanks

440
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:21,840
and then conveyed into the packing house,

441
00:25:21,940 --> 00:25:26,140
where they're cleaned and then sized.

442
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:28,680
These cranberries are destined for processing,

443
00:25:28,681 --> 00:25:32,360
meaning they will end up as juice, sauce, or even craisins,

444
00:25:32,740 --> 00:25:34,460
the cranberry equivalent of raisins.

445
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:40,400
But long before cranberry juice and craisins dominated the market,

446
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:44,400
Native Americans understood the power of the fresh berries,

447
00:25:45,620 --> 00:25:49,140
using them in medicines, poultices, and dyes.

448
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:53,300
Dried berries were even pressed together with venison and fat

449
00:25:53,301 --> 00:25:56,320
to produce pemmican, a survival cake

450
00:25:56,321 --> 00:25:58,500
that kept for long periods of time.

451
00:25:58,680 --> 00:26:03,680
The first commercial growers in the 19th century

452
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:07,680
recruited cheap field laborers, often women and children,

453
00:26:07,780 --> 00:26:11,320
to meticulously hand-harvest the wild-growing vines.

454
00:26:11,420 --> 00:26:13,520
When the men, women, and children

455
00:26:13,620 --> 00:26:15,600
would show up on the cranberry marsh,

456
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:18,200
they were given a measure.

457
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:20,340
Once they had a full measure, they

458
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:22,340
would go over to the edge of the bed,

459
00:26:22,440 --> 00:26:25,240
dump the measure into the receptacle,

460
00:26:25,241 --> 00:26:29,240
the receptacle, and that picker was given a picking chip.

461
00:26:29,241 --> 00:26:33,340
That picking chip would be used for either cash

462
00:26:33,341 --> 00:26:37,520
or merchandise at the company store.

463
00:26:37,521 --> 00:26:40,660
In the 1890s, the first cranberry hand rake

464
00:26:40,661 --> 00:26:43,100
replaced the picker's calloused fingers

465
00:26:43,101 --> 00:26:47,060
and quickly became the harvesting method of choice.

466
00:26:47,061 --> 00:26:49,500
Growers eventually discovered that flooding the cranberry

467
00:26:49,501 --> 00:26:52,140
beds made hand raking much easier.

468
00:26:52,141 --> 00:26:53,200
This is the hand rake.

469
00:26:53,201 --> 00:26:56,440
It's got metal teeth with slots in it.

470
00:26:56,441 --> 00:27:00,000
As you rake, you go through the berries,

471
00:27:00,001 --> 00:27:01,200
through the vines like that.

472
00:27:01,201 --> 00:27:04,920
The berries are floating on top of the water.

473
00:27:04,921 --> 00:27:06,040
They're attached to the vines,

474
00:27:06,041 --> 00:27:08,220
but you still gotta take them off.

475
00:27:08,221 --> 00:27:12,260
And it's a back-breaking job if you have to do it all day long,

476
00:27:12,261 --> 00:27:14,020
especially if the vines aren't trained.

477
00:27:18,060 --> 00:27:21,720
In the 1950s, Wisconsinite Leonard Getzinger

478
00:27:21,721 --> 00:27:25,720
one of the first successful mechanical harvesters.

479
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:29,720
The hand-pushed Getzinger picker uses retractable metal teeth

480
00:27:29,820 --> 00:27:31,720
to comb through the vines and pick berries.

481
00:27:36,760 --> 00:27:39,880
Up until the 1970s, the majority of all cranberries

482
00:27:39,980 --> 00:27:44,380
were sold as fresh fruit for traditional cooking.

483
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:50,480
During the 1970s and 1980s, the juice market exploded,

484
00:27:50,481 --> 00:27:57,200
And the demand for cranberry juice far exceeded the demand for fresh cranberries.

485
00:27:57,540 --> 00:28:02,220
The whole industry shifted from fresh fruit to processed fruit.

486
00:28:02,580 --> 00:28:06,940
Starting in the 70s and 80s, most farmers began wet harvesting with beaders

487
00:28:06,941 --> 00:28:10,480
to bring in a larger, yet less carefully handled crop.

488
00:28:12,500 --> 00:28:16,940
But today, one man feels there is still a better mousetrap for farmers

489
00:28:16,941 --> 00:28:19,140
who raise cranberries for processing.

490
00:28:19,141 --> 00:28:22,140
It's called the Ruby Slipper.

491
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:26,340
It's a machine I invented trying to find a simpler,

492
00:28:26,440 --> 00:28:30,480
faster method to harvest cranberries that was easier

493
00:28:30,580 --> 00:28:33,580
on the fruit, easier on the vines.

494
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:35,080
This is a lot gentler.

495
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:37,600
The bar itself is traveling at a slower speed when

496
00:28:37,700 --> 00:28:39,100
it contacts the vine.

497
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:41,600
But the ground speed of the machine is a lot faster.

498
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,300
The Ruby Slipper is pulled through the marsh

499
00:28:47,301 --> 00:28:50,300
and requires no moving parts to remove the cranberries.

500
00:28:53,300 --> 00:28:56,300
Regardless of the machine, harvesting cranberries for juice

501
00:28:56,400 --> 00:28:59,040
or craisins is not a gentle affair.

502
00:29:04,060 --> 00:29:06,960
But there's another harvest, more steeped in tradition,

503
00:29:07,060 --> 00:29:09,760
that demands a more delicate touch.

504
00:29:09,860 --> 00:29:12,100
Habelman Brothers in Tomah, Wisconsin,

505
00:29:12,200 --> 00:29:17,300
is one of the world's largest producers of fresh cranberries.

506
00:29:17,301 --> 00:29:22,300
This harvester is all about delivering the perfect berry.

507
00:29:22,301 --> 00:29:27,300
Habelman's harvesters utilize stainless steel teeth to comb

508
00:29:27,301 --> 00:29:32,300
the vines, much like the original Getzinger pickers.

509
00:29:32,301 --> 00:29:35,300
But these gentler, modern harvesters use a series of

510
00:29:35,301 --> 00:29:38,300
paddles to free the berries from the vines.

511
00:29:38,301 --> 00:29:41,300
The berries never end up back in the water.

512
00:29:41,301 --> 00:29:44,300
They go right into the boats and then into the trucks.

513
00:29:44,301 --> 00:29:47,400
We can do about 10 acres a day with the two machines

514
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:48,600
that we're using right now.

515
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:54,940
When it's time for packing, the berries go through a separator

516
00:29:55,040 --> 00:29:58,640
line to remove discolored and poor quality berries.

517
00:29:58,740 --> 00:30:02,120
At the front end is an antique device that has never been

518
00:30:02,220 --> 00:30:06,480
upgraded or redesigned, the Bailey separator.

519
00:30:06,580 --> 00:30:10,320
Invented by the H.R. Bailey company in 1917,

520
00:30:10,420 --> 00:30:13,800
it was conceived with the idea that only fresh cramping

521
00:30:13,801 --> 00:30:15,640
fresh cranberries will bounce.

522
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:18,160
So they pass through a series of boards.

523
00:30:18,620 --> 00:30:20,820
Good ones jump and land on a finish belt.

524
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:23,360
The bad ones just kind of filter down the machine.

525
00:30:23,560 --> 00:30:26,380
And a lot of companies have tried to come up with a better design,

526
00:30:26,540 --> 00:30:28,420
but there isn't any out there yet.

527
00:30:30,220 --> 00:30:32,120
The cranberries that passed Bailey's tests

528
00:30:32,121 --> 00:30:34,560
are then double-checked by more modern equipment.

529
00:30:35,480 --> 00:30:37,180
Two different electronic separators

530
00:30:37,181 --> 00:30:39,660
use ultraviolet and fluorescent light

531
00:30:39,661 --> 00:30:42,200
to detect inferior color and rot.

532
00:30:42,201 --> 00:30:48,200
After the antique and high-tech separators have their go,

533
00:30:48,300 --> 00:30:50,940
the final quality check is human.

534
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:53,440
Any bad berries that have survived the gauntlet

535
00:30:53,540 --> 00:30:55,440
are weeded out before packaging.

536
00:30:57,380 --> 00:30:59,380
As the demand for healthier food and drink

537
00:30:59,480 --> 00:31:01,820
continues to spiral, a wild assortment

538
00:31:01,920 --> 00:31:05,120
of cranberry harvesting machines will continue to prowl

539
00:31:05,220 --> 00:31:07,260
the marshes of central Wisconsin.

540
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:17,200
In the low desert of Southern California's Coachella Valley,

541
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:20,600
there's an annual ritual that is perhaps the most dangerous

542
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:21,700
harvest in America.

543
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:30,760
This man is a palmero.

544
00:31:30,860 --> 00:31:33,960
His job is to harvest dates from trees that can grow to heights

545
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:36,760
of more than 80 feet.

546
00:31:36,860 --> 00:31:40,760
Date grower Dennis Jensen speaks of the industry's anxiety

547
00:31:40,761 --> 00:31:43,100
over the latter harvest.

548
00:31:43,101 --> 00:31:44,600
It's dangerous work up there.

549
00:31:44,601 --> 00:31:47,300
One slip and they could end up in a world of hurt.

550
00:31:47,301 --> 00:31:50,300
As much as we try safety-wise,

551
00:31:50,301 --> 00:31:53,800
it seems that every other year we lose someone

552
00:31:53,801 --> 00:31:56,800
to this terrible tragedy.

553
00:31:56,801 --> 00:31:59,220
Some of the other items that are very dangerous

554
00:31:59,221 --> 00:32:01,420
are the spines, the palm fronds themselves

555
00:32:01,421 --> 00:32:04,220
that can pierce your hand or an eye or an arm

556
00:32:04,221 --> 00:32:06,260
or a leg or foot, and they're located

557
00:32:06,261 --> 00:32:07,760
throughout the crown of that tree.

558
00:32:07,761 --> 00:32:08,960
So there's a lot of different dangers

559
00:32:08,961 --> 00:32:10,460
that they have to work with.

560
00:32:12,560 --> 00:32:14,960
The palmeros in this field are hand-harvesting

561
00:32:15,060 --> 00:32:17,960
a premium date known as the medjool,

562
00:32:18,060 --> 00:32:21,000
one of several date varieties that have been cultivated

563
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:22,600
since before recorded time.

564
00:32:26,820 --> 00:32:29,820
Dates are likely the oldest of all harvested fruits.

565
00:32:32,320 --> 00:32:35,020
Nearly 6,000 years ago, they were cultivated

566
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:38,720
in the region of Mesopotamia, or what is now modern

567
00:32:38,721 --> 00:32:42,720
Some traditions trace the fruit to the Garden of Eden.

568
00:32:42,820 --> 00:32:45,720
Even the Bible refers to the land of milk and honey,

569
00:32:45,820 --> 00:32:48,720
and we've been told by Jewish scholars that the honey it was

570
00:32:48,820 --> 00:32:51,460
referring to was the honey of the date palms.

571
00:32:51,560 --> 00:32:54,360
Its ability to grow and produce fruit in such an arid

572
00:32:54,460 --> 00:32:58,980
environment made the date palm a sacred tree.

573
00:32:59,080 --> 00:33:02,780
Dates keep very well, and in that hot desert area where they

574
00:33:02,880 --> 00:33:06,220
had no refrigeration, they could keep them for a long time.

575
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:08,480
And early on, they just kept them

576
00:33:08,481 --> 00:33:13,480
to get rid of air and so forth so that they would keep.

577
00:33:13,580 --> 00:33:16,480
After many failed attempts, date palm offshoots

578
00:33:16,580 --> 00:33:20,720
were successfully imported and planted in the Arizona desert

579
00:33:20,820 --> 00:33:23,720
in the year 1900.

580
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:27,760
The coveted medjool offshoots came from Morocco in 1927

581
00:33:27,860 --> 00:33:32,000
and were later planted in the Coachella Valley.

582
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:35,040
Today, California growers of medjools

583
00:33:35,041 --> 00:33:38,640
and the other major variety, the deglet noor,

584
00:33:38,740 --> 00:33:41,680
oversee a thriving industry that annually harvests

585
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:43,780
a crop worth around $50 million.

586
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:49,180
And it's not just the fruit that is valuable.

587
00:33:49,280 --> 00:33:51,520
The date palm is considered so beautiful

588
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:54,920
that as many as 15,000 trees are harvested and sold

589
00:33:55,020 --> 00:33:58,400
each year to real estate developers.

590
00:33:58,500 --> 00:34:02,160
But times are changing for the skilled palmeros.

591
00:34:02,161 --> 00:34:05,560
Most date growers are relying less on the ladder harvest

592
00:34:05,660 --> 00:34:09,560
and more on a mechanical harvesting aid, the canasta.

593
00:34:13,200 --> 00:34:16,900
Mounted on a nine-ton lift, the platform opens and closes

594
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:18,400
around the date palm's trunk.

595
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:24,620
Even as mechanization makes the work safer and easier,

596
00:34:24,720 --> 00:34:27,220
the job still requires a trained palmero

597
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:30,920
to distinguish dates that are ripe for picking.

598
00:34:30,921 --> 00:34:34,720
Normally what they're going to do is they'll be looking for the ripest ones,

599
00:34:34,721 --> 00:34:36,620
and they're pretty easy to pull off.

600
00:34:36,621 --> 00:34:39,860
And sometimes you can just shake them and they'll actually fall off of the bunches.

601
00:34:39,861 --> 00:34:42,800
But they'll be pulling out the ripe ones, which are the darker color ones.

602
00:34:42,801 --> 00:34:45,160
There's yellow ones on here, which are not ripe yet.

603
00:34:45,161 --> 00:34:48,840
It'll take another probably two to three weeks before they will ripen up.

604
00:34:50,840 --> 00:34:55,940
Prior to harvesting, the palmeros wrap each and every growing date bunch in a nylon bag.

605
00:34:55,941 --> 00:35:02,040
Our date bags are used to keep moisture away from the dates

606
00:35:02,041 --> 00:35:03,880
should we have a sudden thunderstorm.

607
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:07,760
And the rain and moisture causes a lot of decay for the dates.

608
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:11,820
Additional purposes of these bags are to keep insects away

609
00:35:11,821 --> 00:35:14,020
and to keep birds from eating the dates.

610
00:35:17,720 --> 00:35:19,900
To prevent damage to the delicate fruit,

611
00:35:20,400 --> 00:35:22,640
harvesters lower small baskets to the ground

612
00:35:22,641 --> 00:35:24,660
with their hands sorted for ripeness.

613
00:35:25,940 --> 00:35:30,940
After a short journey to Dennis Jensen's Seaview Packing House,

614
00:35:31,040 --> 00:35:32,940
the dates are cooled.

615
00:35:33,040 --> 00:35:34,940
Then they're carefully washed and dried

616
00:35:35,040 --> 00:35:36,940
before they're hand-sorted for packing.

617
00:35:40,940 --> 00:35:43,940
One of five quality categories are assigned before the dates

618
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:45,940
are shipped to points around the world.

619
00:35:45,941 --> 00:35:46,840
around the world.

620
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:58,280
Welcome back to Harvesting Plus.

621
00:35:58,380 --> 00:36:00,620
Today, Kennett Square, Pennsylvania

622
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:03,620
is known as the mushroom capital of the world.

623
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:06,200
And not long ago, the world's largest mushroom grower

624
00:36:06,300 --> 00:36:10,060
was Creekside Mushrooms, located near Pittsburgh.

625
00:36:10,160 --> 00:36:13,240
Creekside owners have since moved on to other ventures,

626
00:36:13,241 --> 00:36:16,780
But for many years, its 800 acres of limestone tunnels

627
00:36:16,880 --> 00:36:19,280
burrowed beneath trees and pasture land

628
00:36:19,380 --> 00:36:22,380
was a fungus farmer's fantasy.

629
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:24,280
The neat thing about growing mushrooms

630
00:36:24,380 --> 00:36:27,680
at this particular facility is that the temperature is pretty

631
00:36:27,780 --> 00:36:30,500
much consistent all year round.

632
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:33,500
And we maintain about a 62 degree temperature all year

633
00:36:33,600 --> 00:36:37,300
round and about a 95% humidity, which allows

634
00:36:37,400 --> 00:36:40,540
mushrooms to just recycle themselves pretty much

635
00:36:40,640 --> 00:36:43,240
every 24 hours.

636
00:36:43,241 --> 00:36:47,240
Here the fungus of choice is the sylvan white agaricus,

637
00:36:47,340 --> 00:36:49,240
the ever-popular white button mushroom.

638
00:36:52,240 --> 00:36:58,180
A small army of harvesters speeds from one cave to the next,

639
00:36:58,280 --> 00:37:03,200
each worker aiming to pick up a minimum of 55 pounds an hour.

640
00:37:06,240 --> 00:37:09,900
Mushrooms are grown in and harvested from large wooden

641
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:10,900
trays.

642
00:37:10,901 --> 00:37:15,200
each tray is harvested in passes or breaks.

643
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:19,300
The first break yields the large and medium mushrooms.

644
00:37:19,400 --> 00:37:22,840
Removing the larger fungi allows more room

645
00:37:22,940 --> 00:37:25,540
for the smaller mushrooms to grow and develop.

646
00:37:25,640 --> 00:37:29,420
I'm picking the mushrooms that are ready

647
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:33,420
at the prime time for harvest, and that depends

648
00:37:33,520 --> 00:37:37,800
upon the stem length, the shape of the cap,

649
00:37:37,801 --> 00:37:40,200
whether it's domed or oval.

650
00:37:40,201 --> 00:37:42,500
If you notice, the only smalls that I take

651
00:37:42,501 --> 00:37:46,540
are the ones that come off basically by accident.

652
00:37:46,541 --> 00:37:48,340
If they're left on there for tomorrow,

653
00:37:48,341 --> 00:37:52,240
there'll be mushrooms that will be harvested again tomorrow.

654
00:37:52,241 --> 00:37:54,180
Each tray ultimately yields

655
00:37:54,181 --> 00:37:57,920
approximately 250 pounds of organic mushrooms.

656
00:37:57,921 --> 00:37:59,920
But not every mushroom is perfect.

657
00:37:59,921 --> 00:38:03,460
This mushroom in particular has a blemish on it.

658
00:38:03,461 --> 00:38:06,300
That is what we consider a number two.

659
00:38:06,301 --> 00:38:10,200
it would not look as nice when it hits the supermarket.

660
00:38:12,840 --> 00:38:15,140
The popular white agaricus mushroom

661
00:38:15,141 --> 00:38:18,180
was first domesticated in the cellars and sewers

662
00:38:18,181 --> 00:38:19,900
of 19th century France.

663
00:38:20,580 --> 00:38:23,520
The process of producing the spawn, or seed,

664
00:38:23,900 --> 00:38:25,780
was the key to growing the fungus commercially.

665
00:38:28,380 --> 00:38:30,800
After the French perfected a strain of spawn,

666
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:32,680
it was soon exported to England

667
00:38:32,681 --> 00:38:35,020
and ultimately to the United States.

668
00:38:36,300 --> 00:38:40,300
The mushroom business took off in 1904 when the United States

669
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:43,300
Department of Agriculture perfected a domestically bred

670
00:38:43,400 --> 00:38:46,740
spawn, one of the very same strains that are used today.

671
00:38:48,940 --> 00:38:52,280
Even with a healthy spawn, profitability growing mushrooms

672
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:56,060
in vast numbers is a very demanding craft.

673
00:38:56,160 --> 00:39:00,960
The process begins with piles of hay, straw, and manure.

674
00:39:01,060 --> 00:39:04,060
This compost is turned, adding water and fresh air

675
00:39:04,061 --> 00:39:06,060
to speed up decomposition.

676
00:39:10,060 --> 00:39:14,060
The compost is then pasteurized in a fresh air tunnel system.

677
00:39:14,061 --> 00:39:17,060
It kills all the other microorganisms that are in the compost,

678
00:39:17,061 --> 00:39:21,060
so that when we do plant the spawn or the mushroom seeds

679
00:39:21,061 --> 00:39:26,060
into the tray, it gives a nice clean medium for the spawn

680
00:39:26,061 --> 00:39:31,060
to take hold inside the compost.

681
00:39:31,061 --> 00:39:33,060
Each tray is filled with compost,

682
00:39:33,061 --> 00:39:34,860
and the spawn is then added.

683
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:37,960
The trays are transported deep within the pitch-dark limestone

684
00:39:38,060 --> 00:39:42,060
caves to begin coaxing the root structure, or mycelia,

685
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:43,060
to the surface.

686
00:39:48,500 --> 00:39:50,720
This tray of organic white agaricus

687
00:39:50,820 --> 00:39:53,020
will begin harvesting around day 15.

688
00:39:56,720 --> 00:39:59,720
Its many mushrooms then travel to a packing facility,

689
00:39:59,721 --> 00:40:04,060
facility where they're weighed, packaged, or sometimes sliced

690
00:40:04,061 --> 00:40:07,120
before they're cooled and quickly shipped to market.

691
00:40:08,900 --> 00:40:13,180
We don't get a whole lot of lead time, nor do we want a whole lot of lead time

692
00:40:13,181 --> 00:40:17,620
because what we produce today, we want to make sure we ship within the next 24 hours.

693
00:40:19,540 --> 00:40:23,420
While the mushroom harvest may seem stable, its future is not.

694
00:40:23,980 --> 00:40:27,300
People that get out of high school today look for other opportunities,

695
00:40:27,301 --> 00:40:30,400
so it gets harder and harder to find labor.

696
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:35,260
Today, mushroom growers have only 75% of the workforce

697
00:40:35,261 --> 00:40:37,500
needed to harvest at full capacity.

698
00:40:38,980 --> 00:40:42,000
Mushrooms are not the only crop impacted by a lack of labor.

699
00:40:42,760 --> 00:40:45,660
The trend is affecting harvests across the nation.

700
00:40:47,440 --> 00:40:49,680
There's certainly less labor available.

701
00:40:50,320 --> 00:40:51,800
It began several years ago,

702
00:40:51,940 --> 00:40:55,580
but the increased pressure to reduce illegal immigration

703
00:40:55,581 --> 00:40:58,040
across the southern United States border

704
00:40:58,041 --> 00:41:00,160
has influenced the amount of laborers

705
00:41:00,161 --> 00:41:01,400
that are available for harvest.

706
00:41:04,180 --> 00:41:06,040
As workers become scarcer,

707
00:41:07,440 --> 00:41:09,060
mechanization will play a larger

708
00:41:09,061 --> 00:41:12,360
but probably still restricted role in the harvest.

709
00:41:13,320 --> 00:41:15,820
Labor is the most important factor in this business.

710
00:41:16,500 --> 00:41:19,720
When you look at the fragility or the nature of the fruit,

711
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:21,740
this is not something that is conducive

712
00:41:21,741 --> 00:41:23,860
to mechanical harvesting.

713
00:41:25,580 --> 00:41:27,940
We rely very heavily on a labor force that's skilled

714
00:41:28,040 --> 00:41:30,020
to discern fruit quality on the tree,

715
00:41:30,120 --> 00:41:33,120
and there's really no other way to go about doing that.

716
00:41:35,620 --> 00:41:38,520
Truly, our machines will become more sophisticated

717
00:41:38,620 --> 00:41:41,620
and sensitive, and a declining labor force

718
00:41:41,720 --> 00:41:45,440
will spur that mechanical innovation.

719
00:41:45,540 --> 00:41:48,640
But only time will tell if the machine will ever match

720
00:41:48,740 --> 00:41:51,980
the human hand's ability to delicately pluck

721
00:41:52,080 --> 00:41:53,540
an unblemished piece of fruit.

